on-arrival: polarization += 5; caballeristas_dissent -= 10; pce_relation += 5; dlr_relation -= 5; prr_relation -= 2; ar_relation += 4; prrs_relation += 5; catholics_psoe -= 7; workers_psoe += 5; rural_psoe += 5; pro_republic += 3; middle_ap += 3; workers_ap += 5

The Guardia Civil detain Cardinal Segura at the border and escort him out of the country. The expulsion is receivied with joy from the anti-clerical workers and radical republicans, who celebrate the government's firmness.

However, the Catholic right portrays Segura as a martyr of religious persecution, accelerating the radicalization of conservative voters toward more conservative movements.

@diplomatic_solution
title: Seek diplomatic channels with the Vatican
subtitle: Request Segura's replacement through Nuncio Tedeschini without forcible expulsion.
on-arrival: polarization -= 7; caballeristas_dissent += 10; pce_relation -= 5; cnt_relation -= 5; dlr_relation += 5; prr_relation += 2; ar_relation -= 4; prrs_relation -= 5; catholics_psoe += 10; workers_psoe -= 5; rural_psoe -= 5; diplomat_solution = 1; pro_republic -= 3

The government avoids dramatic physical expulsion, opting instead to negotiate privately with Pius XII. Left republicans like Azaña feel that this is giving concessions to the right. 

While the Poped eventually recalls Segura to Rome, our restraint infuriates the more radical members of the PSOE and UGT, who accuse the cabinet of weakness toward reactionary discourses.